{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016DvoqEu1zX7bWE3Fq8PNs4","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Meet Hankweave \n     2→\n     3→\n     4→The official documentation for Hankweave—a runtime for reliable, brownfield AI engineering.\n     5→\n     6→These docs were built by Hankweave. So were the diagrams. \n     7→\n     8→We're eating our own cooking, and soon, you will too. \n     9→\n    10→---\n    11→\n    12→## What is Hankweave?\n    13→\n    14→Hankweave freezes ephemeral agentic behaviors into declarative, reproducible programs. When your agent fails on run #47, you don't debug \"emergent behavior.\" You trace the exact sequence of events, roll back to a checkpoint, and fix it.\n    15→\n    16→The core abstraction is the **codon**—an atomic unit of agent work with a prompt, a model, and tracked files. Codons sequence into **hanks** (JSON programs). Between codons, you get git checkpoints, event logs, and clear boundaries. Problems in codon 3 don't leak into codon 7.\n    17→\n    18→For the full picture, start with [Codons](/concepts/codons) and [Hanks](/concepts/hanks).\n    19→\n    20→---\n    21→\n    22→## Reading the Docs\n    23→\n    24→The documentation is organized into four sections:\n    25→\n    26→| Section | What You'll Find |\n    27→|---------|------------------|\n    28→| **Guides** | Step-by-step tutorials—start here if you're new |\n    29→| **Concepts** | How Hankweave works: codons, hanks, loops, checkpoints, sentinels |\n    30→| **Reference** | CLI, WebSocket protocol, configuration, state machine specs |\n    31→| **Use Cases** | Production patterns and real-world examples |\n    32→\n    33→### Recommended Reading Order\n    34→\n    35→**Just getting started (Running Hanks):**\n    36→\n    37→1. [Getting Started](/guides/getting-started) — Install and run your first hank\n    38→2. [Codons](/concepts/codons) — The atomic unit of agentic work\n    39→3. [Hanks](/concepts/hanks) — How codons compose into programs\n    40→\n    41→**Building Hanks:**\n    42→\n    43→- [Building a Hank](/guides/building-a-hank) — The CCEPL development loop\n    44→- [Loops](/concepts/loops), [Rigs](/concepts/rigs), [Sentinels](/concepts/sentinels) — Advanced constructs\n    45→\n    46→**Debugging Hanks:**\n    47→\n    48→- [Debugging](/guides/debugging) — Every tool call is logged. The answer is in there.\n    49→\n    50→**Building on top of Hanks:**\n    51→\n    52→- [WebSocket Protocol](/reference/protocol) and [Client Libraries](/reference/client-libraries)\n    53→\n    54→\n    55→---\n    56→\n    57→## About Southbridge\n    58→\n    59→Hankweave is built by [Southbridge](https://www.southbridge.ai). \n    60→\n    61→We build infrastructure for AI systems that need to keep working six months from now—the infra for reliable, brownfield AI engineering. \n    62→\n    63→Check out our website - maybe start with: \n    64→- [CCEPL-Driven Development](https://www.southbridge.ai/blog/ccepl-driven-development)\n    65→- [Antibrittle Agents](https://www.southbridge.ai/blog/antibrittle-agents)\n    66→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A78iR7WiT1gpFyqhqnoErX","session_id":"c6374b5f-86c6-4583-a2d5-1355f78fbe1d","uuid":"98ef849a-de0d-4cfc-8b9f-01f7be420986","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/source/hankweave-docs/README.md","content":"# Meet Hankweave \n\n\nThe official documentation for Hankweave—a runtime for reliable, brownfield AI engineering.\n\nThese docs were built by Hankweave. So were the diagrams. \n\nWe're eating our own cooking, and soon, you will too. \n\n---\n\n## What is Hankweave?\n\nHankweave freezes ephemeral agentic behaviors into declarative, reproducible programs. When your agent fails on run #47, you don't debug \"emergent behavior.\" You trace the exact sequence of events, roll back to a checkpoint, and fix it.\n\nThe core abstraction is the **codon**—an atomic unit of agent work with a prompt, a model, and tracked files. Codons sequence into **hanks** (JSON programs). Between codons, you get git checkpoints, event logs, and clear boundaries. Problems in codon 3 don't leak into codon 7.\n\nFor the full picture, start with [Codons](/concepts/codons) and [Hanks](/concepts/hanks).\n\n---\n\n## Reading the Docs\n\nThe documentation is organized into four sections:\n\n| Section | What You'll Find |\n|---------|------------------|\n| **Guides** | Step-by-step tutorials—start here if you're new |\n| **Concepts** | How Hankweave works: codons, hanks, loops, checkpoints, sentinels |\n| **Reference** | CLI, WebSocket protocol, configuration, state machine specs |\n| **Use Cases** | Production patterns and real-world examples |\n\n### Recommended Reading Order\n\n**Just getting started (Running Hanks):**\n\n1. [Getting Started](/guides/getting-started) — Install and run your first hank\n2. [Codons](/concepts/codons) — The atomic unit of agentic work\n3. [Hanks](/concepts/hanks) — How codons compose into programs\n\n**Building Hanks:**\n\n- [Building a Hank](/guides/building-a-hank) — The CCEPL development loop\n- [Loops](/concepts/loops), [Rigs](/concepts/rigs), [Sentinels](/concepts/sentinels) — Advanced constructs\n\n**Debugging Hanks:**\n\n- [Debugging](/guides/debugging) — Every tool call is logged. The answer is in there.\n\n**Building on top of Hanks:**\n\n- [WebSocket Protocol](/reference/protocol) and [Client Libraries](/reference/client-libraries)\n\n\n---\n\n## About Southbridge\n\nHankweave is built by [Southbridge](https://www.southbridge.ai). \n\nWe build infrastructure for AI systems that need to keep working six months from now—the infra for reliable, brownfield AI engineering. \n\nCheck out our website - maybe start with: \n- [CCEPL-Driven Development](https://www.southbridge.ai/blog/ccepl-driven-development)\n- [Antibrittle Agents](https://www.southbridge.ai/blog/antibrittle-agents)\n","numLines":66,"startLine":1,"totalLines":66}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T13:00:11.898Z"}
